{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a9cd36e05e3f7a1c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# LangFlow: Continuous Diffusion Rivals Discrete in Language Modeling\narXiv: 2604.11748\nAuthors: Yuxin Chen, Chumeng Liang, Hangke Sui, Ruihan Guo, Chaoran Cheng, Jiaxuan You, Ge Liu\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We connect embedding-space diffusion language models to Flow Matching via Bregman divergence, yielding a theoretically grounded cross-entropy training objective.\n  2. We derive a novel ODE-based negative log-likelihood bound for principled evaluation of continuous flow-based language models.\n  3. We propose an information-uniform noise scheduling principle, which motivates a learnable Gumbel distribution-based noise scheduler tailored for language data.\n  4. We reveal and rectify the training protocol for self-conditioning in continuous diffusion, showing it improves both likelihood and sample quality unlike in discrete diffusion.\n  5. We introduce LangFlow, the first continuous diffusion language model to rival and surpass discrete counterparts on large-scale language modeling benchmarks.\n---\n# UAT: Unified Audio-Text Diffusion for Audio Generation, Editing, and Captioning\narXiv: 2606.04939\nAuthors: Hui Wang, Yifan Yang, Zeyue Tian, Yuhang Jia, Jinghua Zhao, Long Zhou, Bing Han, Cheng Liu, Jiaming Zhou, Geng Tu, Yong Qin\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "471692a6-2dfb-4ca5-a80a-333a8cea46c9", "uuid": "f566b6d4-22b1-44d3-aaef-b06dd2358452", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T07:07:44.941Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# LangFlow: Continuous Diffusion Rivals Discrete in Language Modeling\narXiv: 2604.11748\nAuthors: Yuxin Chen, Chumeng Liang, Hangke Sui, Ruihan Guo, Chaoran Cheng, Jiaxuan You, Ge Liu\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We connect embedding-space diffusion language models to Flow Matching via Bregman divergence, yielding a theoretically grounded cross-entropy training objective.\n  2. We derive a novel ODE-based negative log-likelihood bound for principled evaluation of continuous flow-based language models.\n  3. We propose an information-uniform noise scheduling principle, which motivates a learnable Gumbel distribution-based noise scheduler tailored for language data.\n  4. We reveal and rectify the training protocol for self-conditioning in continuous diffusion, showing it improves both likelihood and sample quality unlike in discrete diffusion.\n  5. We introduce LangFlow, the first continuous diffusion language model to rival and surpass discrete counterparts on large-scale language modeling benchmarks.\n---\n# UAT: Unified Audio-Text Diffusion for Audio Generation, Editing, and Captioning\narXiv: 2606.04939\nAuthors: Hui Wang, Yifan Yang, Zeyue Tian, Yuhang Jia, Jinghua Zhao, Long Zhou, Bing Han, Cheng Liu, Jiaming Zhou, Geng Tu, Yong Qin\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
